<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Tobago</h1><p class="st-meta">1628 CE – 1677 CE (approx.) · capital Lampsinsburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Contested Dutch colony on Tobago, named New Walcheren by Zeeland settlers and fought over by the Dutch, Courlanders, English and French.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-zeeland">Zeeland</a>, led by Jan de Moor, sent settlers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> in 1628 under a patent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> and called the island New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walcheren">Walcheren</a>. The first colony was destroyed by the Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> in 1637, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> returned in the 1650s under the Lampsins brothers, displacing the rival colony of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony centred on Lampsinsburg and a cluster of forts and plantations growing tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> with the labour of enslaved Africans. In 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-lampsins">Cornelis Lampsins</a> Baron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, a rare French title over a Dutch-run island. The settlers were mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Reformed Protestants, with French, Jewish and other incomers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> changed hands repeatedly during the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Franco-Dutch wars. In 1677 a French fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ii-destrees">Jean d'Estrées</a> attacked, and a magazine explosion destroyed the Dutch fort and its defenders. The French razed the colony, and Dutch rule ended; the island stayed thinly settled until the British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-tobago">Colony of Tobago</a> of 1763.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-tobago.jpg" alt="Dutch Tobago" width="320" height="253"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Bowen derivative work: MagentaGreen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tobago_subdivision_1779_BOWEN_color_B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1628 CE – 1677 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lampsinsburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cornelis Lampsins, Jacob Binckes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Walcheren, Nieuw-Walcheren</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1652&amp;focus=dutch-tobago" aria-label="Dutch Tobago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3003 1760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Tobago lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3323 2016h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3323 2016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Trinidad and Tobago. <a href="/atlas?year=1652&amp;focus=dutch-tobago">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

